#public(unit) {

// Sections 16-21: declarations and scope.

render_section_16 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso has no `let`, `var`, or `const` keyword. A declaration is a name,
        \\an optional type annotation, and an initialiser, and the punctuation
        \\between them carries the whole meaning: whether the name is immutable,
        \\whether its value is required at compile time, and whether the type is
        \\written or inferred.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\A colon introduces the type annotation. When the right side is an
        \\ordinary value expression, a single `:` or a `::` before it binds a
        \\constant; an `=`, `:=`, or `::=` binds something that can be written
        \\to. A declaration with a type and no initialiser is zero-filled. A
        \\definition-shaped right side—such as `struct`, `#import`, or a
        \\procedure—is the named-definition exception described below.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    LIMIT :: 64;
        \\    step ::= 2;
        \\    total := 0;
        \\    scale: f64 = 1.5;
        \\    seen: s64;
        \\    total = LIMIT / step;
        \\    print("total=% scale=% seen=%\n", total, scale, seen);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\total=32 scale=1.5 seen=0
    );

    subheading(4, "The declaration forms");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The same eight value-binding spellings are accepted at file scope and
        \\inside a procedure body, and two of them mean different things in the
        \\two places. The difference is that only a declaration inside a body is
        \\entered into the checker's compile-time environment, so only a local
        \\value binding can be a compile-time binding. In the first eight rows,
        \\`v` means an ordinary value expression.
    );
    table_header(6, "Spelling   —   At file scope   —   In a body");
    table_row(7, "`x :: v`   |   Constant.   |   Constant.");
    table_row(8, "`x : T : v`   |   Typed constant.   |   Typed constant.");
    table_row(9, "`x : T :: v`   |   Typed constant.   |   Typed constant.");
    table_row(10, "`x ::= v`   |   Mutable global.   |   Compile-time binding.");
    table_row(11, "`x : T := v`   |   Mutable global.   |   Typed compile-time binding.");
    table_row(12, "`x := v`   |   Mutable global.   |   Mutable local.");
    table_row(13, "`x : T = v`   |   Mutable global.   |   Mutable local.");
    table_row(14, "`x : T;`   |   Zero-filled global.   |   Zero-filled local.");
    table_row(47, "`x :: definition`   |   Named definition.   |   Named definition; supported nested kinds are hoisted.");
    table_row(48, "`x := definition`, `x ::= definition`, `x : D : definition`, or `x : D :: definition`   |   Named definition; its declaration type is inferred, or checked against `D`.   |   Not the named-definition grammar; `:=` and `::=` take the ordinary local-binding path.");
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\Both `:` and `::` are accepted after a type annotation, so `MAX: ssize :
        \\100` and `MAX: ssize :: 100` declare the same typed constant. Every
        \\ordinary value-binding form in the first eight rows ends with `;`,
        \\both at file scope and in an ordinary procedure body. A named
        \\definition instead follows its own grammar: a braced definition ends
        \\at `}`, while a definition such as `distinct` or `#import` carries its
        \\own `;`. Inside a `#code` quotation, the parser also lets a binding
        \\immediately before any closing `}` end there, including at the end of
        \\a nested block. Statements and Semicolons gives the complete rule.
    );
    note(16, #string,oneline
        \\For an ordinary value expression, `::=` is the one spelling whose
        \\meaning flips between the two scopes. A file-scope `G ::= 7;` is an
        \\ordinary mutable global, not a compile-time constant, and a
        \\`#if G == 7` that follows it fails with `error: unknown const G`. A
        \\definition-shaped right side takes the named-definition path instead.
        \\Use `::` for a file-scope value constant.
    );

    subheading(17, "Constants");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`::` means an immutable binding. It comes in two flavours, and the
        \\compiler chooses between them without any help from the source: if the
        \\initialiser can be evaluated during checking the name becomes a
        \\compile-time constant, and if it cannot the name becomes a
        \\runtime-frozen constant instead. A `::` whose initialiser is not
        \\compile-time evaluable is not an error.
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\A runtime-frozen constant is lowered like an ordinary local: the
        \\initialiser is evaluated once, at the declaration, and the name is
        \\bound to that result for the rest of the scope. Later changes to
        \\whatever the initialiser read do not reach it.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n := 7;
        \\    FROZEN :: n * 2;
        \\    print("FROZEN=%\n", FROZEN);
        \\    n = 100;
        \\    print("FROZEN=%\n", FROZEN);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\FROZEN=14
        \\FROZEN=14
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The two flavours differ in exactly one visible way: a frozen constant
        \\is not a compile-time value, so it cannot drive a construct that
        \\demands one. Adding `#if FROZEN == 14` to the program above gives
        \\`error: unknown const FROZEN`, `#assert #comptime FROZEN == 14` gives
        \\`error: #assert #comptime condition is not compile-time evaluable`, and
        \\passing it to a `#comptime` parameter gives
        \\`error: argument for #comptime parameter 'v' must be known at compile time`.
        \\Each of those carries `note: unknown const FROZEN`.
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Neither flavour has storage. `p := *FROZEN` is
        \\`error: cannot take a pointer to a non-addressable expression: 'FROZEN'`,
        \\and the same holds for a file-scope constant.
    );

    subheading(24, "Compile-time bindings");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\Inside a body, `x ::= v` and `x: T := v` declare a compile-time
        \\binding. The initialiser must be compile-time evaluable, the name is
        \\registered in the compile-time environment, and the binding may be
        \\updated by ordinary assignment during checking. This is what drives
        \\`#inline while` counters and static branching.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    width ::= 4;
        \\    width = width * 2;
        \\    #if width == 8 print("the static branch saw %\n", width);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\the static branch saw 8
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\An initialiser the checker cannot fold is rejected rather than frozen:
        \\`K ::= n * 2` over a runtime `n` is
        \\`error: comptime variable initializer must be compile-time evaluable`.
        \\A compile-time binding also has no runtime storage, so its address
        \\cannot be taken: `p := *width` is `error: cannot take pointer to width`.
        \\Values with no runtime representation must be bound this way or with
        \\`::`. That includes values of type `type`, `Namespace`, `Library`,
        \\`Code`, and `Pattern`, as well as typeclass and macro declarations.
    );

    subheading(29, "Variables");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\`x := v` and `x: T = v` declare a mutable binding with storage. `:=`
        \\infers the type from the initialiser; the annotated form fixes it and
        \\checks the initialiser against it. `x: T;` declares a mutable binding
        \\with no initialiser, and the storage is zero-filled rather than left
        \\holding whatever was on the stack. A declaration with neither a type
        \\nor an initialiser has nothing to work from and is rejected.
    );

    subheading(31, "Assigning to a constant");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Assignment to a constant is always rejected, but the diagnostic
        \\depends on where the constant was declared and how it was spelled. A
        \\file-scope constant is not in the assignable namespace at all, so name
        \\resolution fails before the assignment rule is ever consulted; a local
        \\constant is found and then refused, with a help line naming the
        \\spelling that would have made it mutable.
    );
    table_header(33, "Binding   —   Assigning to it");
    table_row(34, "`K :: 7;` at file scope   |   `error: unbound 'K'`");
    table_row(35, "`MAX: ssize : 100;` at file scope   |   `error: unbound 'MAX'`");
    table_row(36, "`c :: 9;` in a body   |   `error: constant cannot be modified`, with `help: change '::' to ':=' to make the binding mutable`");
    table_row(37, "`c: s64 : 9;` in a body   |   the same error, with `help: change ':' to '=' to make the binding mutable`");
    table_row(38, "`c: s64 :: 9;` in a body   |   the same error, with `help: change '::' to '=' to make the binding mutable`");
    table_row(39, "`g := 0;` at file scope   |   Accepted; a global is an ordinary mutable binding.");
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\The refusal reaches through members and indexes, not just through the
        \\bare name. For `p :: Point.{ .x = 1, .y = 2 };` the statement `p.x =
        \\5;` is the same `error: constant cannot be modified`, reported at the
        \\field, and for `a: [3]s64 :: .[1, 2, 3];` so is `a[0] = 9;`.
    );

    subheading(41, "`#undefined` initialisers");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\`x: T = #undefined;` declares a binding whose initial contents are not
        \\defined. It exists to skip the zero fill for a buffer that is about to
        \\be overwritten in full, which is why the standard library's binary
        \\readers open with `bytes: [16]u8 = #undefined;` before reading into
        \\the slice. The spelling is the directive; a bare `undefined` is
        \\`error: unbound 'undefined'`.
    );
    code_block(43, "glosso", #string
        \\zeroed :: () -> u8 {
        \\    b: [64]u8;
        \\    return b[3];
        \\}
        \\
        \\scratch :: () -> u8 {
        \\    b: [64]u8 = #undefined;
        \\    return b[3];
        \\}
    );
    code_block(44, "text", #string
        \\define i8 @glo_zeroed() {
        \\entry:
        \\  %r0 = alloca i64, align 8
        \\  %r1 = alloca [64 x i8], align 1
        \\  %r2 = alloca i64, align 8
        \\  %r3 = alloca i8, align 1
        \\  %temp.mark = load i64, ptr @glo_temp_used, align 8
        \\  store i64 %temp.mark, ptr %r0, align 8
        \\  store [64 x i8] zeroinitializer, ptr %r1, align 1
        \\  store i64 3, ptr %r2, align 8
        \\
        \\define i8 @glo_scratch() {
        \\entry:
        \\  %r0 = alloca i64, align 8
        \\  %r1 = alloca [64 x i8], align 1
        \\  %r2 = alloca i64, align 8
        \\  %r3 = alloca i8, align 1
        \\  %temp.mark = load i64, ptr @glo_temp_used, align 8
        \\  store i64 %temp.mark, ptr %r0, align 8
        \\  store i64 3, ptr %r2, align 8
    );
    paragraph(45, #string,oneline
        \\Under `--backend llvm-ir -O0` the two bodies differ by exactly one
        \\instruction: `zeroed` emits
        \\`store [64 x i8] zeroinitializer, ptr %r1` and `scratch` does not.
        \\`#undefined` has no standalone type of its own. It takes the type of
        \\the declaration, parameter, or field that receives it; bound alone with
        \\`x := #undefined` it falls back to the `label` type used for an absent
        \\jump target.
    );
    note(46, #string,oneline
        \\`#undefined` changes nothing at file scope. A global lives in the
        \\program image and has to be given some pattern of bytes, so
        \\`buffer: [8]u8 = #undefined;` still emits
        \\`@glo_global_buffer = internal global [8 x i8] zeroinitializer`. The
        \\directive only pays for itself on a local.
    );
}

render_section_17 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A Glosso source file is a flat sequence of top-level declarations.
        \\There is no package statement, no header, and no forward-declaration
        \\form, because there is nothing to declare forward: the whole file is
        \\read before anything in it is checked.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Order is therefore free. `main` may be the first declaration in the
        \\file and call a procedure written below it, a global may be typed by a
        \\struct declared later, and `#import` and `#load` may sit at the very
        \\bottom.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("later() = %\n", later());
        \\    print("LIMIT = % origin.x = %\n", LIMIT, origin.x);
        \\}
        \\
        \\LIMIT :: 64;
        \\Point :: struct { x: ssize; y: ssize; }
        \\origin: Point = .{ .x = 3, .y = 4 };
        \\later :: () -> ssize { return LIMIT / 2; }
        \\
        \\#import "Io";
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\later() = 32
        \\LIMIT = 64 origin.x = 3
    );

    subheading(4, "What may appear at file scope");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The parser reads the name first and then decides from the token that
        \\follows it. The punctuation shown below is part of each complete
        \\top-level declaration. A declaration that does not end with its own
        \\braced definition body must end with `;`. A procedure with a body, an
        \\aggregate, a typeclass, an instance, or a bare `#comptime { ... }` step
        \\ends when its own body closes and is not followed by `;`. Feature
        \\section directives, top-level `#memory` overlays, and the less common
        \\typed `#comptime -> T { ... }` form accept an optional trailing
        \\semicolon.
    );
    table_header(6, "Form   —   Declares");
    table_row(7, "`name :: expr;`   |   A constant.");
    table_row(8, "`name : T : expr;`, `name : T :: expr;`   |   A typed constant.");
    table_row(9, "`name := expr;`, `name ::= expr;`   |   A mutable global, type inferred, when the right side is an ordinary expression.");
    table_row(10, "`name : T = expr;`, `name : T := expr;`   |   A mutable global with a written type.");
    table_row(11, "`name : T;`   |   A zero-filled global.");
    table_row(12, "`name :: (params) -> R { … }`   |   A procedure.");
    table_row(13, "`name :: struct { … }`, `union { … }`, `enum { … }`, `enum_flags T { … }`   |   An aggregate type.");
    table_row(14, "`name :: distinct T;`   |   A nominal type.");
    table_row(15, "`name :: typeclass (…) { … }`   |   A typeclass.");
    table_row(16, "`head ::: instance Class { … }`   |   An instance; `:::` is its dedicated separator.");
    table_row(51, "`name := definition`, `name ::= definition`   |   A named type, procedure, namespace, library, or macro with its declaration type inferred.");
    table_row(52, "`name : D : definition`, `name : D :: definition`   |   The same named definitions with declaration-value type `D` checked explicitly.");
    table_row(17, "`name :: #fn_ptr(…) -> R;`   |   A function-pointer type.");
    table_row(18, "`name :: #library[,kind] \"file\";`   |   A foreign library handle.");
    table_row(19, "`#import \"M\";`, `name :: #import \"M\";`   |   A module import.");
    table_row(20, "`#load \"path\";`, `name :: #load \"path\";`   |   An isolated relative module; use `#load,embed` for a shared source fragment.");
    table_row(21, "`#thread_local <global declaration>`   |   A thread-local global.");
    table_row(22, "`#comptime { … }`, `#comptime call();`   |   A compile-time step; the expression form requires `;`.");
    table_row(23, "`#insert <Code-or-string expression>;`   |   Declarations generated during checking.");
    table_row(24, "`#memory name { … }`   |   A memory contract attached to a function declared elsewhere.");
    table_row(25, "`#enable(feature);`, `#disable(feature);`   |   A feature gate, sticky to the end of the file.");
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\`#if`, `else #if`, and `else` also select declarations at module scope.
        \\The condition must be a compile-time boolean. Only the chosen branch adds
        \\declarations or loads dependencies, so an inactive platform branch need not
        \\have its files or foreign libraries installed. Custom operator declarations
        \\and dependencies that provide operator syntax must stay unconditional,
        \\because parsing precedes branch selection. `#assert` remains a statement;
        \\use a `#comptime` block for a file-level assertion.
    );
    subheading(46, "Named definitions are values too");
    paragraph(47, #string,oneline
        \\At file scope, a definition may use the traditional `name :: definition` spelling,
        \\an inferred `name := definition` or `name ::= definition`, or an
        \\explicit declaration-value annotation written `name : D : definition`
        \\(with `::` also accepted for the second separator). The parser
        \\recognises a definition-shaped right side before deciding that `:=`
        \\means a mutable global. Structs, unions, enums, flag enums, distinct
        \\types, typeclasses, procedures, imports, loads, libraries, and
        \\function-pointer definitions all take this route. Inside a procedure
        \\body, only the canonical `name :: definition` form enters the nested
        \\definition path; the alternate punctuation above is file-scope syntax.
    );
    code_block(48, "glosso", #string
        \\Point : type : struct { x: ssize; y: ssize; }
        \\double : (ssize) -> ssize : (value: ssize) -> ssize { return value * 2; }
        \\Io : Namespace : #import "Io";
        \\kernel32 : Library : #library,system "kernel32";
        \\Show_One : Typeclass(type) : typeclass(T: type) {
        \\    show_one :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\trace : Macro((ssize) -> void) : (value: ssize) #expand { _ := value; }
        \\Auto_Point := struct { x: ssize; }
        \\auto_main ::= () {}
    );
    paragraph(49, #string,oneline
        \\The annotation describes the value introduced by the declaration, not
        \\the values stored inside it. An aggregate, distinct type, or
        \\function-pointer definition has type `type`; `#import` and `#load`
        \\produce `Namespace`; `#library` produces `Library`; a typeclass uses
        \\the parameterized declaration annotation
        \\`Typeclass(parameter-kinds...)`; a macro uses
        \\`Macro((parameter-types...) -> result)`; and an ordinary procedure has
        \\its procedure type. `Typeclass(...)` and `Macro(...)` are recognized
        \\in this declaration-annotation position; they are not generally
        \\callable type constructors in ordinary expressions. These names are
        \\case-sensitive. The former lowercase `namespace`, `library`, and
        \\`pattern` spellings are ordinary identifiers, not builtin types.
    );
    note(50, #string,oneline
        \\The checker verifies an explicit declaration-value annotation. For
        \\example, `Wrong : Library : struct {}` reports `declaration 'Wrong'
        \\has type type, not declared type Library`. In an explicit declaration
        \\annotation, `Typeclass` requires at least one parameter kind and
        \\`Macro` requires exactly one procedure signature, such as
        \\`Macro((ssize) -> void)`.
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\File scope also collects declarations that were not written there. A
        \\`name :: (…)`, `struct`, `union`, `enum`, or `distinct` declaration
        \\written inside a procedure body is hoisted out to file scope and is
        \\visible to every other top-level declaration. Blocks, Scope, and
        \\Shadowing covers that rule and what it does to name lookup.
    );

    subheading(28, "Globals and their initialisers");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\A global is a mutable binding with static storage. Its name is private
        \\by default; use `#public` or `#public(unit)` when another source file
        \\needs it. Its initializer is evaluated during compilation and emitted as
        \\the symbol's static initial value. Visibility and Ownership Units covers
        \\which callers can reach the binding.
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\That is a hard requirement, not an optimisation. An initialiser the
        \\checker cannot fold is an error, and the most common way to hit it is
        \\to read another global. A global variable is not a compile-time value,
        \\so `second: s64 = first + 1;` gives `error: unknown const first`.
        \\Anything that needs the running program is rejected the same way:
        \\`buffer: ?*void = alloc(64);` is
        \\`error: #magic intrinsic 'ctx_alloc' is not available during compile-time evaluation`.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\Anything the compile-time evaluator can run is allowed, including
        \\calls to procedures declared later in the file and constants declared
        \\later in the file.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\compute :: () -> s64 { return 6 * 7; }
        \\
        \\answer: s64 = compute();
        \\table: [4]s64 = .[1, 2, 3, 4];
        \\blank: s64;
        \\
        \\main :: () { x := answer + table[0] + blank; }
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\@glo_global_answer = internal global i64 42
        \\@glo_global_blank = internal global i64 0
        \\@glo_global_table = internal global [4 x i64] [i64 1, i64 2, i64 3, i64 4]
    );

    subheading(34, "Initialisation order");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Because every global initial value is a folded constant, there is no
        \\runtime global-initialisation pass at all. Nothing runs before `main`
        \\to fill globals in, so there is no initialisation order to specify and
        \\no order-of-initialisation hazard between files. The guarantee the
        \\compiler makes is that every global holds its declared initial value,
        \\or zero, from the first instruction of the program.
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The visible consequence is that a side effect in a global initialiser
        \\happens during the build, not during the run.
    );
    code_block(37, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\noisy :: () -> s64 { print("initializer ran\n"); return 1; }
        \\
        \\value: s64 = noisy();
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("value=%\n", value); }
    );
    code_block(38, "text", #string
        \\glosso: compile program.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\initializer ran
        \\glosso: link program.exe (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\glosso: done program.exe  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\value=1
    );
    note(39, #string,oneline
        \\`initializer ran` is printed by the compiler, on the compiler's
        \\stdout, between the `compile` and `link` progress lines. Work that has
        \\to happen once at program start belongs at the top of `main` or behind
        \\an explicit initialisation procedure, not in a global initialiser.
    );

    subheading(40, "Thread-local storage");
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\`#thread_local` prefixes a global variable declaration and gives each
        \\thread its own copy of the storage. Everything else about the
        \\declaration is unchanged: the initialiser is still folded at compile
        \\time, and an omitted initialiser still means zero.
    );
    code_block(42, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#thread_local depth: s64 = 0;
        \\#thread_local scratch: [4]u8;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    depth += 1;
        \\    scratch[0] = 7;
        \\    print("depth=% scratch[0]=%\n", depth, scratch[0]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(43, "text", #string
        \\depth=1 scratch[0]=7
    );
    paragraph(44, #string,oneline
        \\The directive prefixes a variable and nothing else. `#thread_local K
        \\:: 5;` and `#thread_local f :: () { }` are both
        \\`error: #thread_local can only prefix a global variable declaration`,
        \\and inside a body it is not a statement at all:
        \\`error: unexpected directive 'thread_local'`.
    );
    note(45, #string,oneline
        \\Whether `#thread_local` reaches the object file depends on the target.
        \\On a hosted target the global is emitted as
        \\`@glo_global_depth = internal thread_local global i64 0`; on Windows,
        \\on freestanding Linux x86-64, and on bare metal the backend drops the
        \\`thread_local` qualifier and emits an ordinary internal global,
        \\because those targets do not use the ELF thread-local model the
        \\runtime is built around. Targets and Cross-Compilation covers the
        \\target list.
    );
    table_row(53, "`Type ::: [#public] { ... }`   |   An inherent method or associated-function block.");
    table_row(54, "`#public`, `#public(unit)`, `#private`   |   A declaration visibility prefix or transparent group.");
    table_row(55, "`#if condition { ... } else { ... }`   |   Compile-time selection of module declarations.");
}

render_section_18 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`place = value;` is a statement, not an expression. It has no result
        \\and it cannot be nested: `y := (x = 2);` is
        \\`error: unexpected token '='`, and so is `if x = 1 { }`. An assignment
        \\written where a comparison was meant is a parse error rather than a
        \\silent truth test. The update-assignment operators described below are
        \\a separate mechanism and do produce values.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The type rule is the ordinary conversion rule, with no relaxation for
        \\assignment. The right side must already be, or implicitly convert to,
        \\the type of the left side. Widening between integer types is implicit;
        \\narrowing is not, so `small: s32 = 1; big: s64 = 2; small = big;` is
        \\`error: assign s64 to s32`. An unrelated type is the same shape of
        \\message: assigning a `string` to an `s32` is
        \\`error: assign string to s32`. Conversions: What Is Implicit gives the
        \\full rule, and Relaxing Strictness with `lossy_numerics` gives the
        \\gate that turns the narrowing case back on.
    );

    subheading(2, "What can be assigned to");
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The left side must denote storage. Four forms do: a mutable name,
        \\a field of one, an element of one, and the target of a pointer
        \\dereference. They compose, and an element reached through a view
        \\writes through to the array the view was taken from.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    arr: [4]s64 = .[1, 2, 3, 4];
        \\    arr[1] += 10;
        \\
        \\    x := 5;
        \\    p := *x;
        \\    p.* += 2;
        \\
        \\    view := arr[0..2];
        \\    view[0] += 100;
        \\
        \\    print("arr = % % % %   x = %\n", arr[0], arr[1], arr[2], arr[3], x);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(5, "text", #string
        \\arr = 101 12 3 4   x = 7
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\Anything else is `error: left side of assignment is not assignable`.
        \\That covers a literal (`5 = 3;`), the result of a call (`f() = 3;`),
        \\and any computed expression (`(a + b) = 3;`). Constants get their own
        \\diagnostics, listed in Values, Constants, and Variables. Parameters,
        \\by contrast, are ordinary mutable bindings and may be assigned to; the
        \\caller's value is unaffected because arguments are passed by value.
    );

    subheading(7, "Assignment copies");
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\Assignment of an aggregate copies it. A struct or fixed array bound
        \\from another one is an independent value, and writing through the
        \\copy does not disturb the original. Views and pointers are the
        \\exception, and only because the thing being copied is the reference,
        \\not what it refers to.
    );
    code_block(9, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Point :: struct { x: ssize; y: ssize; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Point = .{ .x = 1, .y = 2 };
        \\    b := a;
        \\    b.x = 99;
        \\    print("a.x=% b.x=%\n", a.x, b.x);
        \\
        \\    c: [3]s64 = .[1, 2, 3];
        \\    d := c;
        \\    d[0] = 99;
        \\    print("c[0]=% d[0]=%\n", c[0], d[0]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\a.x=1 b.x=99
        \\c[0]=1 d[0]=99
    );

    subheading(11, "Update assignment");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\Every `op=` spelling is a genuine operator, declared in the Prelude as
        \\a typeclass method carrying `#operator(assign, 0)`. Level 0 is the
        \\loosest tier on the precedence scale, so everything on the right of it
        \\groups first, and the result is stored back into the left operand.
        \\Because it is an operator rather than statement syntax,
        \\`y := (x += 1);` compiles and binds `y` to the updated value.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    total := 10;
        \\    total += 3;
        \\    total <<= 2;
        \\
        \\    saturated: u8 = 250;
        \\    saturated +|= 10;
        \\
        \\    wrapped: u8 = 250;
        \\    wrapped +%= 10;
        \\
        \\    ready := false;
        \\    ready ||= total > 0;
        \\
        \\    print("total=% saturated=% wrapped=% ready=%\n",
        \\          total, saturated, wrapped, ready);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\total=52 saturated=255 wrapped=4 ready=1
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\There are nineteen of them, and each has its own two-parameter
        \\typeclass with an `Output` associated type. None of those classes has
        \\to be implemented directly: the Prelude ships a blanket instance for
        \\every one, conditioned on the corresponding binary operator class, so
        \\a type that implements `Add` gets `+=` for free.
    );
    table_header(16, "Operator   —   Typeclass   —   Blanket instance requires");
    table_row(17, "`+=`   |   `AddAssign`   |   `Add(L, R)`");
    table_row(18, "`-=`   |   `SubAssign`   |   `Sub(L, R)`");
    table_row(19, "`*=`   |   `MulAssign`   |   `Mul(L, R)`");
    table_row(20, "`/=`   |   `DivAssign`   |   `Div(L, R)`");
    table_row(21, "`%=`   |   `RemAssign`   |   `Rem(L, R)`");
    table_row(22, "`+%=`   |   `WrapAddAssign`   |   `WrapAdd(L, R)`");
    table_row(23, "`-%=`   |   `WrapSubAssign`   |   `WrapSub(L, R)`");
    table_row(24, "`*%=`   |   `WrapMulAssign`   |   `WrapMul(L, R)`");
    table_row(25, "`+|=`   |   `SaturatingAddAssign`   |   `SaturatingAdd(L, R)`");
    table_row(26, "`-|=`   |   `SaturatingSubAssign`   |   `SaturatingSub(L, R)`");
    table_row(27, "`*|=`   |   `SaturatingMulAssign`   |   `SaturatingMul(L, R)`");
    table_row(28, "`<<=`   |   `ShiftLeftAssign`   |   `ShiftLeft(L, R)`");
    table_row(29, "`<<|=`   |   `SaturatingShiftLeftAssign`   |   `SaturatingShiftLeft(L, R)`");
    table_row(30, "`>>=`   |   `ShiftRightAssign`   |   `ShiftRight(L, R)`");
    table_row(31, "`&=`   |   `BitAndAssign`   |   `BitAnd(L, R)`");
    table_row(32, "`|=`   |   `BitOrAssign`   |   `BitOr(L, R)`");
    table_row(33, "`^=`   |   `BitXorAssign`   |   `BitXor(L, R)`");
    table_row(34, "`&&=`   |   `LogicalAndAssign`   |   `LogicalAnd(L, R)`");
    table_row(35, "`||=`   |   `LogicalOrAssign`   |   `LogicalOr(L, R)`");
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\A type with no instance reports the missing operator rather than a
        \\type mismatch: `a: Point; b: Point; a += b;` is
        \\`error: no operator '+=' for Point, Point`. The Operator System and
        \\Assignment Operators cover declaring new ones and what `#operator(assign,
        \\0)` means for parsing.
    );
    note(37, #string,oneline
        \\The wrapping, saturating, and shifting families exist because the
        \\plain arithmetic operators trap on overflow at `-O0` and `-O1`. `+%=`
        \\wraps and `+|=` clamps, which is why `250 +%= 10` is `4` and `250 +|=
        \\10` is `255` on a `u8`. Overflow, Traps, and the `-O` Level explains
        \\when the trap is emitted.
    );

    subheading(38, "Binding several names at once");
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\A declaration may take a comma-separated list of names on the left,
        \\which destructures a multiple-result call or a tuple. `_` in that list
        \\discards a component. This is a declaration form, not an assignment
        \\form: `::`, `:=` and `::=` all accept a name list.
    );
    code_block(40, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\divmod :: (a: ssize, b: ssize) -> (ssize, ssize) { return a / b, a % b; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    q, r := divmod(7, 2);
        \\    print("q=% r=%\n", q, r);
        \\
        \\    _, remainder := divmod(9, 4);
        \\    print("remainder=%\n", remainder);
        \\
        \\    pair := .(1, "two");
        \\    n, s := pair;
        \\    print("n=% s=%\n", n, s);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(41, "text", #string
        \\q=3 r=1
        \\remainder=1
        \\n=1 s=two
    );
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\There is no multiple-assignment statement. Once the names exist,
        \\`a, b = divmod(9, 4);` is `error: unexpected token '='`, and so is the
        \\swap idiom `x, y = y, x;`. The right side of a destructuring
        \\declaration is one expression, not a list: `a, b := 1, 2;` is
        \\`error: unexpected token ','`, and the tuple literal `a, b := .(1, 2);`
        \\is the spelling that works.
    );
    paragraph(43, #string,oneline
        \\The name count must match what the right side produces:
        \\`a, b, c := divmod(7, 2);` is
        \\`error: destructuring binds 3 names from a 2-value result`. Function
        \\Declarations covers multiple and named results, and Tuples covers the
        \\values themselves.
    );
}

render_section_19 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A block is a brace-delimited statement list used as a statement in its
        \\own right. It introduces a scope: a name declared inside it is
        \\unreachable after the closing brace. Scoping is lexical and
        \\sequential, so a name is visible from its declaration to the end of
        \\the scope that holds it, and not before.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := 1;
        \\    {
        \\        print("inner sees outer a=%\n", a);
        \\        a := 99;
        \\        print("inner a=%\n", a);
        \\    }
        \\    print("outer a=%\n", a);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\inner sees outer a=1
        \\inner a=99
        \\outer a=1
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The first `print` resolves `a` to the outer binding because the inner
        \\declaration has not been reached yet, and the inner `a := 99;`
        \\declares a second variable rather than assigning to the first, so the
        \\outer one is unchanged once the block ends. Naming a binding after its
        \\scope has closed is `error: unbound 'hidden'`; in call position the
        \\same failure reads `error: unknown function hidden`, because the
        \\checker resolves callees through a lookup separate from the one it
        \\uses for values.
    );
    subheading(4, "What introduces a scope");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\Every construct with a body carries its own scope, and the checker
        \\clones the environment when it enters one. A body written without
        \\braces is still a body: `if true  a := 5;` followed by a use of `a`
        \\on the next line is `error: unbound 'a'`.
    );
    table_header(6, "Construct   —   Scope");
    table_row(7, "`{ ... }` as a statement   |   A fresh scope, closed by the `}`.");
    table_row(8, "A procedure body   |   A fresh scope; the parameters are declared in it.");
    table_row(9, "An `if` or `else` body   |   A fresh scope, whether or not it is braced.");
    table_row(10, "A `while` or `for` body   |   A fresh scope, re-entered on every iteration.");
    table_row(11, "A switch or pattern arm body   |   A fresh scope per arm, holding that arm's captures.");
    table_row(12, "A `#push_context` or `#push_allocator` body   |   A fresh scope.");
    table_row(13, "A lambda body   |   A fresh scope that cannot reach enclosing locals.");
    subheading(14, "Nested declarations do not nest");
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\A `::` declaration written inside a procedure body is not necessarily
        \\local. `name :: (...)`, `name :: struct`, `name :: enum`,
        \\`name :: union` and `name :: distinct` are lifted out of the body by
        \\the parser and become file-level declarations; only constants and
        \\variables such as `name :: 3` and `name := 3` stay local. The
        \\declaration forms themselves are covered in Top-Level Declarations and
        \\Thread-Local Storage.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\collect :: () {
        \\    Sample :: struct { v: ssize; }
        \\    s: Sample = .{ .v = 3 };
        \\    print("collect %\n", s.v);
        \\}
        \\
        \\report :: () {
        \\    t: Sample = .{ .v = 9 };
        \\    print("report %\n", t.v);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () { collect(); report(); }
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\collect 3
        \\report 9
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`Sample` is declared inside `collect` and used from `report`, which
        \\never mentions it. The cost of that convenience is a shared file
        \\namespace: two procedures that each declare a `step :: () -> ssize`
        \\collide with `error: function 'step' is already declared`.
    );
    note(19, #string,oneline
        \\Hoisting happens in the parser, before any `#if` is evaluated. A
        \\procedure declared inside `#if false { ... }` still exists and is
        \\still callable; the static branch removes the statements around the
        \\declaration, not the declaration.
    );
    subheading(20, "Captures and pattern bindings");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\A pattern capture is a binding whose scope is exactly the branch that
        \\proved the pattern. It does not exist before the arm and it does not
        \\survive it.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Res :: union { Ok: ssize; Err: string; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    r := Res.Ok(5);
        \\    if r == {
        \\    case .Ok(v);  print("ok %\n", v);
        \\    case .Err(e); print("err %\n", e);
        \\    }
        \\    if #pattern .Ok(v) = r print("tested v=%\n", v);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\ok 5
        \\tested v=5
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Naming `v` after the switch is `error: unbound 'v'`, with `help: did
        \\you mean 'r'?`. The `else` of an `if #pattern` test cannot see the
        \\captures either: on that path the pattern did not match, so there is
        \\nothing for them to name.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The one form that binds outward is `#pattern P := subject else { ... }`,
        \\which places the captures in the enclosing scope so a procedure is not
        \\indented one level deeper per optional. That is sound only because the
        \\`else` block is checked without the bindings and must not fall
        \\through; the form is covered in Bind or Diverge.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Res :: union { Ok: ssize; Err: string; }
        \\
        \\take :: (r: Res) -> ssize {
        \\    #pattern .Ok(v) := r else { return -1; }
        \\    return v * 10;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", take(Res.Ok(4)));
        \\    print("%\n", take(Res.Err("no")));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\40
        \\-1
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`using p;` inserts a struct's field names into the current scope for
        \\the remainder of it, and they disappear with the scope: reading `x`
        \\after the block that said `using p;` is `error: unbound x`. The
        \\statement is covered in `using`, `#empty`, and `#as`.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\A lambda body is a scope that deliberately cannot reach outward.
        \\Reading an enclosing local gives `error: lambda captures local 'base',
        \\but captured closures are not supported`, with `help: pass the value
        \\as an explicit callback or context argument`. Globals and constants
        \\remain readable; see Lambdas.
    );
    subheading(30, "Shadowing");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\Inside a procedure, shadowing is unrestricted. A local may hide a
        \\parameter, a global variable, a top-level constant, a type name or a
        \\procedure name, and a nested block may hide anything the enclosing
        \\block declared.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\LIMIT :: 100;
        \\counter := 10;
        \\
        \\scale :: (x: ssize) -> ssize {
        \\    x := x * 2;
        \\    LIMIT := 3;
        \\    counter := 0;
        \\    return x + LIMIT + counter;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("scale=% LIMIT=% counter=%\n", scale(4), LIMIT, counter);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", "scale=11 LIMIT=100 counter=10");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\Redeclaring a name in the same block is accepted as well: `a := 1;
        \\a := 2;` is two declarations rather than a declaration and an
        \\assignment, and the second binding wins from that point on. Call
        \\position and value position are resolved separately, so shadowing a
        \\procedure name does not make the procedure uncallable: after
        \\`print := 3;` the call `print("%\n", print);` still resolves and
        \\prints `3`.
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Shadowing is refused within one file's top-level namespace. A second
        \\declaration there is an error, including declarations brought into
        \\the same unit by `#load,embed` or hoisted from a procedure body.
        \\Ordinary `#load` preserves the loaded module's identity. Qualified
        \\loads let a caller distinguish equally named declarations in separate
        \\modules without merging their types or global storage.
    );
    table_header(36, "Duplicate at file scope   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(37, "`K :: 1;` then `K :: 2;`   |   `error: constant 'K' is already declared`");
    table_row(38, "Two procedures named `helper`   |   `error: function 'helper' is already declared`");
    table_row(39, "Two structs named `Point`   |   `error: type 'Point' is already declared`");
    note(40, #string,oneline
        \\The standard library's public names occupy that same namespace. A
        \\top-level `PI :: 3.14;` in a user file is `error: constant 'PI' is
        \\already declared`, because the Prelude already owns the name. Local
        \\bindings are free to shadow it.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\Labels are function-scoped rather than block-scoped: they are
        \\collected in a pre-pass over the whole body, so a jump may go forward,
        \\and may enter a nested block from outside it. See Labels and `goto`.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\A `defer` belongs to the scope it is written in and runs when that
        \\scope is left. See `defer` and `#try defer`.
    );
    bullet(43, #string,oneline
        \\Visibility groups are transparent and restore the enclosing audience when
        \\their braces close. See Visibility and Ownership Units.
    );
}

render_section_20 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Functions, types, constants, globals, methods, and named module bindings are
        \\private by default. A private declaration is accessible only in its defining
        \\physical source file and logical module. Struct fields and enum or union
        \\variants start public, subject to the visibility of their enclosing type.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Use `#public` to expose an API, `#public(unit)` to share declarations within
        \\one ownership unit, and `#private` to make file privacy explicit. Each
        \\directive prefixes one named declaration or a transparent group. Groups
        \\introduce no namespace and restore the enclosing default when they end.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#public Counter :: struct {
        \\    value: s64;
        \\    #private cached: s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Counter ::: #public {
        \\    make :: () -> #Self_Type { return .{ .value = 0, .cached = 0 }; }
        \\    read :: (#self) -> s64 { return #self.value; }
        \\    #private reset_cache :: (*#self) { #self.*.cached = 0; }
        \\    #public(unit) refresh_cache :: (*#self) { #self.*.cached = #self.*.value; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\#public(unit) {
        \\    refresh :: () {}
        \\    #private scratch: s64;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The visibility after `:::` sets an inherent block's default member audience.
        \\`Counter ::: #public { ... }` is equivalent to
        \\`Counter ::: { #public { ... } }`. A method without an explicit audience in
        \\a plain `Counter ::: { ... }` block remains private, even if the type or
        \\surrounding declaration group is public. Put a block visibility before its
        \\optional `where` clause.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Visibility on locals or parameters is rejected. An inherent block takes its
        \\visibility after `:::`, not before the owner type name. Typeclass requirements
        \\and instance slots follow the class contract. A public name beginning with
        \\`_` is still public; its spelling does not hide it from imports.
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The former `#enable(private_section)`, `#disable(private_section)`,
        \\`private_sections`, and `siblings` forms have been removed and produce a
        \\migration diagnostic. Replace them with declaration prefixes or visibility
        \\groups. Other feature gates keep their file-scoped behavior.
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\Imports, loads, and reexports never widen an item's audience. Each module
        \\still declares its own dependencies. `#load,embed` shares a logical module
        \\but preserves each physical file's private identity: declarations used by
        \\another embedded file need `#public(unit)` or `#public`.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\// parts/store.glo
        \\#public STORE_PUBLIC :: 1;
        \\#private key :: 2;
        \\#public(unit) STORE_SHARED :: 3;
        \\#public read_key :: () -> s64 { return key; }
        \\
        \\// app.glo
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#load "parts/store.glo";
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % %\n", STORE_PUBLIC, read_key(), STORE_SHARED);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\In this example both files are in the entry file's project directory and
        \\therefore share an ownership unit by default. The caller can use
        \\`STORE_SHARED` through its explicit load, while `key` remains accessible
        \\only inside `store.glo`. Loading a file alone never makes its private
        \\declarations callable.
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Ownership controls who may add inherent methods and use unit-visible names.
        \\It is separate from module scope. Without explicit mappings, the entry file
        \\and other files under its source directory form a project unit. A configured
        \\`--module-path` directory creates dependency boundaries by its first relative
        \\path component. Other external files get separate file units.
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\Repeated `--unit-source UNIT=FILE` options configure ownership explicitly.
        \\Once any mapping is given, every loaded non-standard source, including the
        \\entry file, requires an exact mapping. Canonical paths keep their unit across
        \\aliases, reexports, and embedded loads. Standard sources belong to the
        \\reserved `builtin:std` unit, and names starting with `builtin:` are reserved.
    );
    code_block(11, "glosso", #string
        \\// app and its implementation share a unit; the dependency has its own.
        \\// glosso --unit-source app=app.glo --unit-source app=parts/store.glo app.glo
    );
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\Private fields cannot be read by a method in another file, even in the same
        \\unit. An inaccessible field also blocks external aggregate literals, spread
        \\construction, and recursive default initialization. Expose a factory such as
        \\`Counter.make()` to construct the value. Copying an existing value and
        \\updating its accessible fields remain permitted.
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\Public signatures cannot expose less-visible types. Generic bodies and
        \\default expressions retain the access rights of their defining file;
        \\specialization does not grant access to the caller's private members.
        \\Reflection omits inaccessible fields and variants without changing layout
        \\or discriminants. Matching a type with hidden variants requires a catch-all.
    );
    note(14, #string,oneline
        \\Language visibility is independent of native symbol export. A defined
        \\`#c_call` function can retain its external linker name while remaining
        \\private to Glosso callers. An ordinary private `main` is a valid entry point;
        \\it does not need a public annotation.
    );
}

render_section_21 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`defer` schedules a statement to run when the enclosing scope is left.
        \\Two forms are accepted: `defer <statement>;` and `defer { ... }`. The
        \\statement is not executed where it is written; it is pushed onto the
        \\scope's pending list and run on the way out.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    defer print("end of main\n");
        \\    {
        \\        defer print("A\n");
        \\        defer print("B\n");
        \\        defer { print("C1\n"); print("C2\n"); }
        \\        print("block body\n");
        \\    }
        \\    print("after block\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\block body
        \\C1
        \\C2
        \\B
        \\A
        \\after block
        \\end of main
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Pending statements run last-in, first-out, so cleanup unwinds in the
        \\reverse of the order it was set up, which is the order that matches
        \\acquisition. The block form is a single entry: `C1` and `C2` run
        \\together, and together they run before `B`.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Each entry belongs to the scope it was written in, not to the
        \\procedure. The inner block's three entries run at its closing brace;
        \\the entry in `main` waits until `main` returns.
    );
    subheading(5, "Which exits run the pending statements");
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i := 0;
        \\    while i < 4 {
        \\        defer print("  defer sees i=%\n", i);
        \\        i += 1;
        \\        if i == 2 continue;
        \\        if i == 4 break;
        \\        print("  body i=%\n", i);
        \\    }
        \\    print("done i=%\n", i);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\  body i=1
        \\  defer sees i=1
        \\  defer sees i=2
        \\  body i=3
        \\  defer sees i=3
        \\  defer sees i=4
        \\done i=4
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\The loop body is a scope, so the entry is registered and discharged
        \\once per iteration: four times, including the iteration cut short by
        \\`continue` and the one cut short by `break`. The printed value is
        \\equally informative. `i` is incremented after the `defer` line, and
        \\the trace shows the incremented value, because a deferred statement is
        \\evaluated when it runs and not when it is written.
    );
    table_header(9, "Leaving the scope by   —   Pending statements");
    table_row(10, "Reaching the closing brace   |   Run.");
    table_row(11, "`return`   |   Run, for every scope between the `return` and the procedure body.");
    table_row(12, "`break` or `continue`   |   Run, for every scope up to and including the loop body.");
    table_row(13, "The end of a loop iteration   |   Run, once per iteration.");
    table_row(14, "`goto`   |   Not run.");
    table_row(15, "`exit()`   |   Not run; the process ends inside the call.");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`goto` transfers to a label rather than unwinding scopes, so it steps
        \\over the pending list entirely. That is why `defer` cannot carry
        \\cleanup in a procedure that uses the jump library for control
        \\transfer; see Explicit Control Transfer with the Jump Library.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Jump";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    {
        \\        defer print("inner defer\n");
        \\        print("before goto\n");
        \\        goto('out);
        \\    }
        \\    'out;
        \\    print("after goto\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\before goto
        \\after goto
    );
    note(19, #string,oneline
        \\Pending statements run before the caller observes the result, and a
        \\result named by a bare local is read after they have run. In
        \\`by_name` below the caller sees `99`; writing `return x + 0;`
        \\materializes a temporary first and yields `1`. Do not assign in a
        \\deferred statement to a local that the same scope returns by name.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\by_name  :: () -> ssize { x := 1; defer x = 99; return x; }
        \\by_value :: () -> ssize { x := 1; defer x = 99; return x + 0; }
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("by_name=% by_value=%\n", by_name(), by_value()); }
    );
    code_block(21, "text", "by_name=99 by_value=1");
    subheading(22, "`#try defer`");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`#try defer <statement>` registers an entry that runs only when the
        \\scope is left along an error path. It is the rollback half of a
        \\two-part cleanup: `defer` releases what was acquired either way, and
        \\`#try defer` undoes work that only a successful completion should
        \\keep.
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\An error path means propagation through postfix `?`, so the form
        \\requires a boundary that `?` can return through. Without one it is
        \\`error: #try defer requires a Try-returning function or an enclosing
        \\#try block`. Propagation itself is covered in `Try`, `?`, and `#try`.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\step :: (fail: bool) -> string!ssize {
        \\    if fail return .Err("step failed");
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\run :: (fail: bool) -> string!ssize {
        \\    print("  acquire\n");
        \\    #try defer print("  rollback\n");
        \\    defer print("  release\n");
        \\    v := step(fail)?;
        \\    print("  commit\n");
        \\    return .Ok(v + 10);
        \\}
        \\
        \\report :: (r: string!ssize) {
        \\    if r == { case .Ok(v); print("  -> ok %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("  -> err %\n", e); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("success path:\n");
        \\    report(run(false));
        \\    print("failure path:\n");
        \\    report(run(true));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\success path:
        \\  acquire
        \\  commit
        \\  release
        \\  -> ok 11
        \\failure path:
        \\  acquire
        \\  release
        \\  rollback
        \\  -> err step failed
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\On the success path `run` reaches `commit`, the plain entry runs, and
        \\the rollback is discarded unexecuted. On the failure path `step`
        \\returns `.Err`, the `?` carries it out of `run` without reaching
        \\`commit`, and both entries run. The order is worth reading: the
        \\rollback was registered first, so it runs last.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Both kinds share one last-in, first-out list. An error-only entry is
        \\skipped on the success path but keeps its position, so the interleaved
        \\order of the two kinds is the reverse of the order they were written
        \\in.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\step :: (fail: bool) -> string!ssize {
        \\    if fail return .Err("boom");
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\run :: (fail: bool) -> string!ssize {
        \\    #try defer { print("  undo 1\n"); }
        \\    defer print("  always 1\n");
        \\    #try defer print("  undo 2\n");
        \\    defer print("  always 2\n");
        \\    v := step(fail)?;
        \\    return .Ok(v);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("ok:\n");
        \\    _ := run(false);
        \\    print("err:\n");
        \\    _ := run(true);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\ok:
        \\  always 2
        \\  always 1
        \\err:
        \\  always 2
        \\  undo 2
        \\  always 1
        \\  undo 1
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\Only propagation through `?` counts as an error path. A hand-written
        \\`return .Err(...);` is an ordinary return: the plain entries run and
        \\the `#try defer` entries do not. A procedure that mixes explicit error
        \\returns with `#try defer` rollback silently skips the rollback on
        \\exactly the paths it was written for.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\run :: (fail: bool) -> string!ssize {
        \\    #try defer print("  rollback\n");
        \\    defer print("  release\n");
        \\    if fail return .Err("explicit");
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("explicit .Err:\n");
        \\    _ := run(true);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\explicit .Err:
        \\  release
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\The boundary may be any `Try`-returning result, including the option
        \\type: in a procedure returning `?ssize`, a `?` applied to a `.None` runs
        \\the error-only entries exactly as an `.Err` does.
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Memory analysis follows both the success and propagation paths of
        \\`?`. A release performed by `#try defer` discharges the obligation
        \\on a propagation exit, while the success path still needs its own
        \\release or ownership transfer. An explicit `.Err` return does not
        \\run that cleanup and may leave a real leak, as below.
    );
    code_block(36, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\Fault :: enum { Bad; }
        \\
        \\work :: (fail: bool) -> Fault!ssize {
        \\    p := (64).alloc();
        \\    #try defer p.free();
        \\    if fail return .Err(.Bad);
        \\    p.free();
        \\    return .Ok(1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () { _ := work(false); }
    );
    code_block(37, "text", #string
        \\warning: [memory.maybe_leak] resource in 'returned.Some' may not be released by 'free' on every path
        \\  --> work.glo:7:10
        \\  |
        \\7 |     p := (64).alloc();
        \\  |          ^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> work.glo:7:10
        \\  |
        \\7 |     p := (64).alloc();
        \\  |          ^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\The warning is accurate here. `return .Err(.Bad);` is not a `?`
        \\propagation, so `#try defer p.free()` never runs on that path and the
        \\allocation really does leak. Replacing it with a plain
        \\`defer p.free();` and dropping the later `p.free();` makes the procedure
        \\correct and the warning disappear. Release obligations are covered in
        \\Ownership and Release Obligations.
    );
    bullet(39, #string,oneline
        \\`defer` accepts any statement, including a call, an assignment or a
        \\block; the block form counts as one entry and its statements run in
        \\written order.
    );
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\A deferred statement reads its operands at the moment it runs, so it
        \\observes every mutation made after it was written.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\A `#try defer` written inside a nested block belongs to that block. It
        \\is discarded when the block is left normally, so a failure that
        \\happens after the block does not run it.
    );
}

}
